{
  "title": "Stall recovery resets the conversation mid-game, breaking prefix \
containment under append-only rendering",
  "description": "_recover_from_stall calls reset_context \
(src/magebench/pilot/pilot_recovery.py:133) after 20 turns without progress. \
That wipes the conversation to 2 messages in the middle of a game.\n\n\
Measured over the 12 append-only games available on 2026-08-17 (the A/B \
appendonly arm at c5c00e8d plus a GRPO loop step at e005b2b3): 6 of 12 break \
strict prefix containment. In game_20260816_211956 the message list goes 52 -> \
2 at decision 26 and again at decision 51; prompt tokens drop 7,264 -> 2,538 \
and 8,175 -> 2,538. The pilot log carries the matching warning: \"Stalled: 20 \
turns without progress, last tools: ['pass_priority'], auto-passing until next \
event\".\n\n\
This is not a regression. The reset has always happened; it was invisible \
under windowed rendering because the context window was already discarding \
history, so a reset looked like more of the same. Defaulting to append-only \
(a0029c31) is what made it observable, because append-only's whole premise is \
that decision k's prompt is decision k-1's prompt plus what happened since.\n\n\
SCOPE. mtg-ac counted this across all 468 recorded games, twice and by \
independent routes: 135 games (28.8%) by emitted events, 137 (29.3%) by \
structure, agreeing per-game on 463 of 468. Mean 0.33 wipes per game, max 3. \
The 4 games where structure exceeds emission are missed emissions; the 1 in \
the other direction is expected from the code, since the stall path emits \
before deciding and skips reset_context when the auto-pass ended the game \
(pilot_recovery.py:135). Of 155 emitted wipes, 154 came from the stall path -- \
the truncation and timeout resets at pilot_recovery.py:58 and :183 are live \
code never reached in practice, so the fix has one target.\n\n\
The mechanism is confirmed rather than inferred: message count immediately \
after each of the 161 structurally detected wipes is exactly 2 -- min, median \
and max, no exceptions -- which is the system message plus the single \
synthetic user message reset_context writes. Nothing else in 468 games \
produces that shape, so the detector has no visible false-positive channel.\n\n\
DO NOT COUNT WIPES WITH check_prefix_containment.py. It asks whether a game is \
one clean append-only sequence, which is also false for every windowed game, \
so over a mostly pre-flip corpus it flags nearly everything and measures \
windowing instead. Windowing falls to a floor and stays (plateau at exactly 62 \
messages); a wipe collapses to single digits. Count transitions where message \
count drops below 8. The containment checker is right for ONE known \
append-only trace and wrong for a mixed corpus -- the same trap as filtering \
on 'prompt tokens strictly increasing', where the obvious tool answers an \
adjacent question.\n\n\
WHY IT IS A CORRECTNESS BUG, not a data-quality one. This framing is mtg-ac's \
and it is sharper than the reward-attribution one this issue was first filed \
with. reset_context replaces history with a single synthetic user message \
(pilot_state.py:87-92). Every token sampled after that point was conditioned \
on a two-message context. Concatenating the game into one flat sequence makes \
the training forward pass recompute those logprobs against the FULL history -- \
a conditioning the sampler never saw -- so the importance ratio is computed \
against the wrong prefix. pipelines/SCHEMA.md already names this failure shape \
for re-tokenized re-renders: self-consistent and wrong. A wipe reaches it by a \
different road.\n\n\
So one FLAT row per game is invalid on ~29% of games rather than merely \
degraded, and it fails silently -- the row builds and it trains.\n\n\
STATUS OF THE TRAINING CONSEQUENCE: closed by segmentation, 2026-08-17. \
verl-train-ff's build_game_rows returns a list of segments and \
_rows_for(layout='game') emits one row per segment. A segment's first prompt \
IS the post-wipe two-message context and grows append-only from there, so the \
row reproduces the wiped conditioning rather than papering over it. Verified \
on game 205851 (85 decisions, 1 reset, 2 segments) against the failure stated \
directly -- for every decision, is the row's prefix before that decision's \
response identical to the prompt the sampler conditioned on? 85 of 85 match, 0 \
differ. The importance ratio is 1 on these games.\n\n\
An earlier revision of this issue said splitting was 'not a rescue while union \
asserts one row per prompt'. That was relayed from mtg-ac and is true of \
verl's v0 AgentLoop path, which this pipeline does not take: rollouts come \
from mage-bench and build_batch assembles tensors directly, so N rows from one \
game is just N rows. The constraint binds the verl-native path in \
docs/verl-native-rollout-proposal.md, where a wipe would be unrepresentable, \
and is now an argument against v0 rather than a limit here.\n\n\
THIS ISSUE STAYS OPEN ANYWAY. The harness still wipes mid-game, still emits no \
marker distinguishing a reset from ordinary history, and any future consumer \
that flattens a game -- the verl-native path, an SFT corpus builder, an \
exported transcript -- reintroduces the same bug with nothing to warn it. The \
training layout defends itself; the data does not describe itself.\n\n\
It also selects. Wiped games average 89.9 LLM calls against 67.2 for games \
that do not wipe, so wipes track length -- which means the 28,672-token cap, \
the filter 'prompt tokens strictly increasing', and a decision horizon all \
select against the same long games, and those are the games where the policy \
survived longest. The sign of that bias flips silently: mtg-d1 measured that \
the current long tail is losses, so today these filters mostly drop bad games \
and look harmless. Training exists to turn long games into wins. When it \
works, the same filter starts dropping the best trajectories, with no code \
change and nothing in the logs marking the moment. Report what each filter \
dropped per step rather than assuming it stayed benign.\n\n\
Worth noting the harness already treats half of this correctly: the auto-pass \
is emitted with harness_action=True (pilot_recovery.py:108) and the comment \
there says training data must be able to find and exclude it, because it is an \
action the policy never selected. The same reasoning applies to the context \
reset that immediately follows, and it carries no marker at all.\n\n\
A second-order effect worth measuring separately: the detector fires because \
the policy passed priority for 20 consecutive turns. On the corpus that \
motivated the strategy prompt, passing on decisions with a playable card is \
the defect being trained away -- so stall frequency is partly a symptom of the \
thing under study, and will change as the policy improves. That also makes the \
stall event a free policy-collapse metric that nothing currently reads: it is \
a direct count of degenerate passing, already logged 154 times, and belongs \
next to the dead-group fraction.\n\n\
TWO KNOCK-ON EFFECTS, both found by mtg-ac and verified here.\n\n\
reset_context also calls seen_oracle_cards.clear() (pilot_state.py:94), so a \
wipe re-shows oracle text the policy has already been shown. Wipes correlate \
with passing behaviour (which triggers them) and with game length. Card-text \
exposure is therefore correlated with the outcome variable BEFORE the planned \
card-text A/B starts, and that A/B must be stratified on wipe count rather \
than folded in with another change.\n\n\
pipelines/SCHEMA.md attributed non-extension of prompt_token_ids to the \
60-message history window, which as of a0029c31 no longer fires by default. \
Left alone, it would have told the next reader that non-extension was solved \
and one row per game was safe. Corrected in the same change that filed this.\n\n\
REPRODUCTION\n\n\
  cd ~/.mage-bench/logs\n\
  for g in game_20260816_211947 game_20260816_211950 game_20260816_211956 \\\n\
           game_20260816_212005 game_20260816_212009 game_20260816_212012; do\n\
    python pipelines/check_prefix_containment.py $g/*_llm_trace.jsonl\n\
  done\n\n\
Each reports FAIL with 2 broken transitions (4 for 211956, which resets \
twice). The six sibling games from the same batch pass clean, 2,558 -> 31,611 \
tokens, generation-prompt suffix [4]. These twelve are known append-only, \
which is what makes the checker the right tool here -- see the warning above \
before generalising this command to a corpus.\n\n\
POSSIBLE FIXES, none chosen here\n\n\
The right answer depends on what the training layout should do with a reset, \
which is owned by the training side rather than the harness. Three options, in \
increasing cost:\n\n\
1. Emit a marker on the reset the way the auto-pass already carries \
harness_action=True, and have the trainer split or drop on it. Cheapest, and \
makes existing behaviour honest rather than changing it.\n\n\
2. Gate training on containment: run check_prefix_containment.py per trace and \
refuse to train on a failing game. Turns a silent data defect into a loud one, \
and is worth doing regardless of which fix lands, because it fails closed.\n\n\
3. Stop resetting: recover from a stall without discarding history. Removes \
the defect at the source, but the reset presumably exists because a stalled \
conversation was re-stalling, so that needs establishing rather than \
assuming.\n\n\
Do not 'fix' this by relaxing the containment check.",
